  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 121 AM CDT Fri Aug 28 2026\n\nWinds have gone calm over much of the forecast area this\nmorning. Clearer skies west of the Fox Valley have helped drop\ndew point depressions down to between 2 to 4 degrees (at the\ntime this discussion was published), whereas lingering clouds \nabove 5,000 feet have kept much of the Chicago Metro area drier.\nReduced visibility is possible this morning, but confidence is \nlower for the Metro. The better chances for patchy fog to \ndevelop around daybreak looks to be around and west of the Fox \nValley or near and south of the Kankakee River Valley\n\nGOES water vapor satellite imagery this morning shows a upper \nlevel trough over the northern Great Lakes slowly moving east, \nas a ridge spreads northward over the intermountain west \n(including a stout MCS moving south down the lee side of the \nridge into Kansas). That ridge will be the focus for the rest of\nthe forecast; but, for today, surface high pressure will create\nfairly quiet weather conditions locally. High temperatures are \nexpected to be in the low 80s, with a weak lake breeze \ndeveloping in the afternoon.\n\nThe ridge is expected to expand eastward tonight and into\nSaturday. This will allow slight perturbations in the height\nfield to move eastward over the weekend. The point of\nuncertainty is where will the ridge axis set up and if the \nshape of the ridge will make it more favorable for showers and \nstorm to move into Illinois. For now, models are suggesting a \nshort wave trough will move through the upper level pattern \nSaturday afternoon through the overnight. Models are still \nsuggesting that the EML plume should be more focused over \nsouthern Wisconsin, but there will be plenty of warm air \nadvection and steepening lapse rates through the afternoon and \nevening for the chances of showers and storms to develop. Model \ntrends will need to be monitored to get a better sense on \ncoverage and any southern terminus for showers/storms.\n\nThat ridge is expected to continue to expand eastward Sunday and\nthrough the next week. No major changes were made to the PoPs\nthe NBM provided as slight/chance wording certainly fits the \nbill for any \"ridge rider\" that might pass over the area. More \nnotably is global and ensemble guidance increasing 850 mb \ntemperatures above 20C by the middle of next week. With dew \npoints increasing into the low 70s, there is a chance for heat \nindices to increase into the upper 90s. September might be\napproaching, but summer is far from over.\n\nDK",
